%I A398299 #29 Aug 18 2026 10:16:15
%S A398299 3,7,13,37,97,163,181,193,277,307,313,331,397,421,433,487,541,571,673,
%T A398299 727,757,853,877,967,1033,1087,1093,1297,1423,1471,1483,1567,1621,
%U A398299 1663,1747,1753,1777,1831,1867,1987,2083,2221,2281,2437,2647,2671,2683,2767,2797,2953,3037,3067
%N A398299 Primes p such that there are at least two Fermat primes F_i <= 65537 such that p*(F_i - 1) + 1 is prime.
%C A398299 For these primes p the map f_k defined in A398282 is noninjective for k = p * 2^r, r >= 1. That is, for k = p * 2^r, r >= 1, there are at least two rings Z/m_1Z and Z/m_2Z such that their unit groups have order k and are isomorphic. See the Englezou link for a proof.
%C A398299 Note that the name is a sufficient but not necessary condition for noninjectivity of f_k for r >= 1. However, it seems plausible that, except for 5, these are the only primes p for which f_k is noninjective, for k as defined above.
%H A398299 Miles Englezou, <a href="/A398299/b398299.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A398299 Miles Englezou, <a href="/A398299/a398299.pdf">Proof of noninjectivity</a>
%F A398299 A398282(primepi(a(n))) = -1.
%e A398299 3 is a term since 3*(3 - 1) + 1 = 7, 3*(5 - 1) + 1 = 13, and 3*(257 - 1) + 1 = 769 are prime.
%e A398299 7 is a term since 7*(5 - 1) + 1 = 29 and 7*(17 - 1) + 1 = 113 are prime.
%t A398299 With[{f = 2^2^Range[0, 4]}, Select[Prime[Range[500]], Count[#*f + 1, _?PrimeQ] >= 2 &]] (* _Paolo Xausa_, Aug 18 2026 *)
%o A398299 (PARI)
%o A398299 lista(n) = my(A = [], F = [3, 5, 17, 257, 65537], P = List()); forprime(p = 2, oo, A = apply(x -> isprime(p * (x-1) + 1), F); if(vecsum(A) >= 2, listput(~P, p); if(#P >= n, return(P))))
%Y A398299 Cf. A019434, A398282.
%K A398299 nonn
%O A398299 1,1
%A A398299 _Miles Englezou_, Jul 25 2026
